 ## Overview
-Quantile Interpolation is a method for interpolating nonparametric probability distributions (PDFs) represented as histograms or Kernel Density Estimates (KDEs) within a volume rendering pipeline. It solves the problem of efficiently computing the uncertainty distribution at arbitrary 3D positions during Direct Volume Rendering (DVR), where traditional convolution-based nonparametric approaches suffer from exponential time complexity [S1]. Unlike its siblings, it preserves the modality of probability distributions and provides a closed-form solution with linear time complexity relative to the number of quantiles, making it feasible for real-time DVR reconstruction [S1]. It builds on [3D Trilinear Interpolation](2613-3d-trilinear-interpolation.md) by extending the spatial interpolation logic to the statistical moments (quantile widths) of the data.
